    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.18506v1</id>
    <title>Agentic induction of common data elements and information extraction from sarcoma case report abstracts</title>
    <updated>2026-11-13T18:23:11Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.18506v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.18506v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Common data elements (CDEs) support clinical data comparability but are usually defined manually. We investigated whether large language model agents could derive a CDE schema from sarcoma case report abstracts and apply it at scale. An agent proposed 72,909 candidate CDEs from 24,588 PubMed abstracts; agentic consolidation yielded 1,570 CDEs. We retained 53 supported by at least 50 abstracts and attempted evidence-linked extraction across the corpus. Processing succeeded for all retained CDEs in 79.2% of abstracts, including responses indicating non-mention. Two human reviewers approved 83.0% of retained CDEs for relevance, 67.9% for clarity, and 56.6% for correctness. In 100 sampled abstracts, exact agreement with the value extracted by the LLM was 94.9% among 1,116 pairs with a non-empty answer (abstract-level bootstrap 95% CI, 93.0-96.4%). Errors concentrated in anatomical and temporal fields. These findings support agent-assisted schema drafting followed by expert curation before large-scale extraction.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-13T18:23:11Z</published>
