    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.18412v1</id>
    <title>LitArc-Bench: Disentangling Text-Specific Reader-Response Dynamics from Position and Lexical Affect</title>
    <updated>2026-11-13T16:55:45Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.18412v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.18412v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>When model predictions track an unfolding human affect trajectory, alignment may reflect narrative position, affective words, or text-specific reader-response dynamics. We introduce LitArc-Bench to separate these explanations: 39 Chinese-language literary excerpts segmented into seven checkpoints, with 430 questionnaires from 81 adults in two classroom groups (9,020 VAD ratings). We compare three future-blind model runs with leave-one-text-out position priors, lexical affect, and human-human references. Position alone reaches r = .470 for arousal, making raw alignment insufficient. Yet all six V/A transition advantages over a joint position-lexicon control have text-bootstrap intervals above zero. Cross-fitted models match the correct human text better than 38 mismatched texts in all nine model-dimension cells (p &lt; .0001); a text-invariant negative control returns chance (p = 1). On human transitions at least 0.5 points against the position trend, models recover 69-76% of valence directions and 53-68% for arousal and dominance. Under crossed participant-text resampling, all nine identity intervals remain above zero; joint-control advantages are robust for valence and directional but less certain for arousal. High shape agreement still coexists with scale bias. LitArc-Bench identifies which components of literary reader response are recoverable beyond shallow explanations and why shape, calibration, and human variability require separate evaluation.</summary>
