    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.18395v1</id>
    <title>Smart Home Agents Make Capable but Unpredictable Grid Operators</title>
    <updated>2026-11-13T16:39:14Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.18395v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.18395v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>AI-enabled smart homes are becoming more common across the grid edge. However, there has not been much work on failure response. This area is critical because smart homes operate on the grid edge where failures can cascade down. If a power outage happens within a smart home, a user has to manually troubleshoot their smart home to include tasks such as power load balance and device recovery. We conduct the first resilience analysis of smart home agents across a full power outage, providing a suite of outage simulations in a live environment that measures resilience before, during, and post power outage. We show that smart home agents can act as grid operators in power outages and identify several new failure categories highlighting the current limitations across all of our models. These findings establish the groundwork for smart home agents as grid operators to increase overall resilience on the grid edge.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.MA" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
