    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.18319v1</id>
    <title>Learning Word-Level Explanations of Text Reuse in Latin Literature with Little Annotation</title>
    <updated>2026-11-13T15:33:43Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.18319v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.18319v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Detecting an intertextual reference is only the first step; philologists also need to know how a later author reworked the source. We explain a reuse pair word by word: each word of the reusing passage is first aligned to its source word, then labeled with the operation that transformed it, such as a copy, a change of inflection, or a substitution. Such word-level operations are expensive to annotate, and only a few corpora annotated at this level exist for text reuse in Latin literature. We describe a procedure that needs only a limited number of annotations: synthetic pairs, built by applying known operations to Latin text, train a first model, and active learning chooses the pairs an annotator corrects. Among several architectures, a model that links and labels together performs best: a pointer network that, for every reuse word, chooses a source word and names the operation in the same step. We show that with active learning, 200 pairs chosen by uncertainty are enough for the model to match one trained on every annotated pair of the 1,490 references of the Loci Similes benchmark. We release our code, word-level predictions for all references, and an interactive demo.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-13T15:33:43Z</published>
