    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.18279v1</id>
    <title>GT-FENG: General Theory of Factual Evaluation and Normative Grounding in Language Models *Author(s)</title>
    <updated>2026-11-13T15:11:11Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.18279v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.18279v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Whether a language model's answer is correct often depends on context: the same value can be right under one jurisdiction, date, or legal definition and wrong under another. We introduce GT-FENG (General Theory of Factual Evaluation and Normative Grounding), the first general framework to unify contextual/temporal correctness and rule-based error attribution across law, policy, and other rule-governed domains. GT-FENG checks answers against the applicable rules and identifies documented rules that match incorrect answers. It shows that when multiple rules give the same value, the answer alone cannot tell which rule the model used. In Medicaid income-limit answers from two models, documenting the asked state's own records reduces the number of wrong answers that match only other states' values by 68-78%, and more than half of stable wrong answers then match two or more documented sources. GT-FENG provides a foundation for evaluating language models in rule-governed domains and for future benchmarks and evaluation toolkits. We release GT-FENG as an open-source Python package, gtfeng.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-13T15:11:11Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>7 pages, 4 tables. Code: https://github.com/VonFlair/gtfeng</arxiv:comment>
