    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.18248v1</id>
    <title>Dancing Tokens on the Vocabulary Network from LLM Generation</title>
    <updated>2026-11-13T14:43:55Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.18248v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.18248v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Large language models (LLMs) generate text one token at a time, yet how a particular generation relates to the geometry learned in pre-training remains unclear. The fundamental explainability of LLMs is elusive. Here, we build a similarity network from the input embeddings of six open LLMs and map generation onto it. The token-embedding network is scale-free while heavy-tailed, as are random embeddings with matched covariance, but far more fragmented. Distillation barely changes this topology because input embeddings are largely inherited. The knowledge space is thus deterministic from pre-training. Consecutive generated tokens are rarely graph neighbours and, in most models, no more often than in shuffled order, so generation is not a walk on the predefined network, although the tokens it visits are concentrated within it. Through the logit lens in a layer- wise manner, tokens settle late in depth in most models, and repetitive models remain confined to small domain-specific sub-graphs. These observables could serve as inference-time monitors of degeneration. We thus provide a fresh perspective on understanding LLM generation in the microscopic way from node or link prediction by graph learning.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cond-mat.dis-nn" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-13T14:43:55Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>24 pages, 6 figures</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Yuan-Chao Hu</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
