    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.18150v1</id>
    <title>Who Should Be on the Panel? Task-Conditioned Persona Selection for LLM Trading Agents</title>
    <updated>2026-11-13T13:25:31Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.18150v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.18150v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Multi-agent LLM systems are built by fixing a cast of roles and then designing how the cast coordinates. Which agents should make up the group for a given task is left to the designer, and the same cast is reused for every task. Financial trading makes the cost of that choice visible: stocks trade differently, and the trading styles suited to one need not suit another. We study task-conditioned persona selection, composing the group from the characteristics of the task it will face, and instantiate it with FinPanel, an LLM multi-agent trading system in which a recruiter selects a panel of named trader personas for each stock from a direction-blind statistical sketch of how the stock trades, each persona gives an independent daily call in its own style, and a manager commits the trade after reading the panel's arguments and their desk-kept, hierarchical track records. Because nothing is returned to the traders, the panel stays the panel that was selected, and it can trade a new stock from an empty record. On eight US stocks over the out-of-knowledge year 2025, against three prior LLM trading agents re-implemented in the same framework and against rule-based, predictive and reinforcement-learning baselines, the selected panel is ahead of a fixed panel of the same size and of the prior agents run as their papers prescribe, with a shallower drawdown than buy-and-hold on every stock; it keeps its performance with no warm-up and with no record at all, whereas the memory-augmented agents' returns swing with their warm-up; and neither more seats nor the manager's reading of the arguments substitutes for selecting the right ones. Who is in the group is a design decision to be made per task, before how the group coordinates.</summary>
    <category term="q-fin.TR" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-13T13:25:31Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Accepted at the AI4F workshop, ICAIF 2026. Code: https://github.com/tinyrolls/FinPanel</arxiv:comment>
