  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.18114v1</id>
    <title>Interpretable weight-space programming builds transparent transformers and brain encoding models</title>
    <updated>2026-11-13T12:36:05Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.18114v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.18114v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Neural networks are powerful predictors, yet their opacity limits their usefulness in science, where the goal is explanation rather than mere prediction. In neuroscience, for example, deep encoding models accurately predict brain activity in response to natural stimuli, but struggle to reveal which stimulus features drive those predictions. Here we introduce interpretable weight-space programming (iWSP), a method in which an LLM agent iteratively builds interpretable models by explicitly programming their weights and features in code, without gradient-based training (backpropagation). Without backpropagation to tune parameters, the agent must draw on its prior knowledge and reason over previous trials to hypothesize, hand-craft, and refine human-interpretable computations mirroring the workflow of a human scientist. LLM audits confirm the interpretability of the hand-crafted models. We first validate iWSP by tasking it with constructing transformers for canonical benchmarks such as arithmetic and sentiment classification, finding that LLM agents can successfully craft compact, accurate networks directly. Next, we apply iWSP to build neural encoding models across vision, language, and audition. In the visual cortex, iWSP outpredicts existing interpretable baselines, uncovers distinct computations across early/higher visual areas, and generalizes to unseen data. In language fMRI, iWSP surfaces a confound in the data and in auditory ECoG, it systematically discovers interpretable acoustic features that predict neural activity. Together, these results establish iWSP as a general paradigm for aligning high predictive performance with human-interpretable scientific discovery.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CE" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
