    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17963v1</id>
    <title>Toward Universal Translatability: On the Statistical Ontology of Generative AI</title>
    <updated>2026-11-13T09:53:27Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17963v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17963v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>This paper argues that the problem of translation functions both as a point of origin and as a normative horizon for contemporary generative AI. In current discourse around ''world models,'' their attainment is framed as the achievement of internal consistency, cross-modal alignment, and physical grounding. Taken together, these demands converge on an ideal of the model as a universal translation machine capable of establishing lossless correspondences across linguistic, visual, and physical representation spaces. I show that this ideal presupposes a metaphysical commitment that I call a statistical ontology: the belief that reality has an underlying, computable statistical structure that can be recovered through sufficient data and modeling, rather than being shaped and accessed through situated human interpretation and labor. Tracing this commitment back to Warren Weaver's 1949 Memorandum on machine translation, I outline how translation was historically recast as code deciphering and, later, as the alignment of probability distributions across structured outputs. Finally, I contrast this paradigm with approaches in Translation Studies that foreground opacity, power asymmetries, and the translator's positionality. I argue that generative AI's statistical ontology is pre-Kantian, pre-critical and obscures the infrastructures and human labor that make machine translatability---as alignment---possible in the first place.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-13T09:53:27Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
      <name>Katia Schwerzmann</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17955v1</id>
    <title>WebArena-Pro: A Heterogeneous, Multimodal, Reproducible Benchmark for Web Agents</title>
    <updated>2026-11-13T09:44:48Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17955v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17955v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Web agents powered by large language and vision-language models are increasingly applied to realistic browser work that spans heterogeneous applications, multimodal content, and stateful workflows. However, existing reproducible web-agent benchmarks cover only a small number of web applications drawn from a few software categories, and restrict modality to text and vision. Live benchmarks broaden site coverage but sacrifice reproducibility, since pages and data drift between runs. Moreover, existing benchmarks do not meaningfully evaluate whether agents can understand and use audio and video content embedded within web tasks. To address these gaps, we introduce WebArena-Pro, a benchmark comprising 300 tasks across 20 self-hosted web applications in six domain categories, spanning distinct interface conventions, workflows, and data models. Across the evaluated agents, the best performance is achieved by both Codex with GPT 6.1 Sol and Claude Code with Claude Opus 5.5 at 62.3%, followed by OpenCode with Kimi 3.1 Pro at 60.7%. The strongest GenericAgent configurations, Gemini 3.1 Pro omni and Gemma4 31B, both achieve 30.3%. Among reproducible, human-curated web agent benchmarks, WebArena-Pro provides the broadest application coverage and the most comprehensive multimodal support to date. The benchmark treats audio and video as core observations alongside text and vision, with dedicated actions for extracting information from each. WebArena-Pro runs each task in isolation and supports reproducible, parallel evaluation. Tasks are authored through a dedicated annotator interface, filtered by LLM-assisted triage, and finally validated by humans before release. Project page: https://webarena-pro.github.io</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
