    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17950v1</id>
    <title>Toward Interference-Agnostic RF Source Separation</title>
    <updated>2026-11-13T09:37:57Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17950v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17950v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Separating a radio-frequency signal of interest from a co-channel interference is mostly addressed with specialized separators, each trained for one pair of signal and interference types and therefore relying on a label that a heavily jammed receiver cannot identify. We show that this reliance is not benign: applied to an interference it was not trained on, such a separator usually fails to recover the signal, and in most of the cases we measure it leaves the signal worse than no processing at all. We then train a single separator over every pair, giving it no indication at test time of which one it faces, on two architectures with no component in common: the convolutional WaveNet of the ICASSP 2024 RF Challenge, and a band-split transformer that we adapt from music source separation. Both behave the same way. At the size of one specialized model, the unified separator stays close to the whole models it replaces, keeping the bit error rate close on almost every pair, better on one and worse on another. On an interference absent from every training set, it does as well as the best specialized model, without anyone having to choose which one to deploy.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
