    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17921v1</id>
    <title>RecMem: Recurrent Memory Compression for Long-Sequence Generative Recommendation</title>
    <updated>2026-11-13T08:59:44Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17921v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17921v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Generative recommendation benefits from long interaction histories, but the cost of processing them directly grows with history length. We propose RecMem, a recurrent memory compression framework guided by three desiderata: space invariance, semantic fidelity, and lifelong evolvability. RecMem compresses historical segments into a fixed set of memory slots using a bifurcated encoder-decoder with shared lower layers and slot-wise forget-gated updates. Recommendation supervision trains the memory to preserve predictive information, while stream-consistency training aligns predictions from sequential and packed processing. By construction, the memory state and decoder context are independent of history length; under contraction and approximate semigroup consistency, we further derive a horizon-independent bound on the state discrepancy between these two processing orders. On MerRec, RecMem uses 16 memory slots and a 216-token decoder context, compared with 1,200 tokens for HSTU-1200, while retaining 87.9% of HSTU-1200's Recall@10 and 97.0% of its Recall@200. It outperforms PHSTU and the evaluated recent-only and retrieval baselines, and analyses of distant history show that archived interactions contribute predictive signal.</summary>
